1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,738
It's game night. You and your friend are one move away from

2
00:00:03,738 --> 00:00:04,930
winning a board game.

3
00:00:05,380 --> 00:00:08,910
To win, you need to roll exactly twelve with two dice.

4
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:11,180
Your friend needs exactly seven.

5
00:00:11,630 --> 00:00:15,110
You each get one roll. So, who is more likely to win?

6
00:00:16,310 --> 00:00:17,698
A lot of people say it's the same.

7
00:00:17,698 --> 00:00:20,037
Twelve is one number, seven is one number.

8
00:00:20,037 --> 00:00:22,230
Every number is just as likely, right?

9
00:00:22,680 --> 00:00:26,140
Wrong. Seven comes up six times more often than twelve.

10
00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:30,540
Six times. Same dice. Same table.

11
00:00:30,540 --> 00:00:31,400
Same luck.

12
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:34,359
If you played this race one hundred times,

13
00:00:34,359 --> 00:00:36,519
your friend would win about seventeen times.

14
00:00:36,519 --> 00:00:37,620
You'd win about three.

15
00:00:38,070 --> 00:00:39,765
That's what probability does.

16
00:00:39,765 --> 00:00:42,012
It takes something that feels like pure luck,

17
00:00:42,012 --> 00:00:44,260
and shows you a hidden pattern underneath.

18
00:00:44,710 --> 00:00:45,975
And here's the promise.

19
00:00:45,975 --> 00:00:48,855
Give me one hour, and starting from absolute zero,

20
00:00:48,855 --> 00:00:51,340
you will finally understand probability.

21
00:00:51,940 --> 00:00:54,487
No scary formulas without pictures.

22
00:00:54,487 --> 00:00:57,050
No skipped steps. Just counting, and seeing.

23
00:00:57,650 --> 00:00:58,530
Let's begin.

24
00:00:59,530 --> 00:01:01,030
Welcome to Math Lab TV.

25
00:01:01,030 --> 00:01:03,170
Where math is something you can see.

26
00:01:03,870 --> 00:01:06,652
Today you will learn what a probability really is,

27
00:01:06,652 --> 00:01:08,580
and how to find one just by counting.

28
00:01:09,030 --> 00:01:13,345
You will see the three little words that power almost every

29
00:01:13,345 --> 00:01:17,390
probability question. Not, or, and and.

30
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:20,943
And you will learn how new information changes the odds,

31
00:01:20,943 --> 00:01:23,840
and how to spot the traps that fool almost everyone.

32
00:01:24,440 --> 00:01:28,400
Before we start, why should you care about probability at all?

33
00:01:28,850 --> 00:01:31,906
Because you already use it, every single day,

34
00:01:31,906 --> 00:01:33,060
without noticing.

35
00:01:33,510 --> 00:01:35,956
When the weather app says seventy percent chance of rain,

36
00:01:35,956 --> 00:01:38,940
and you decide to take an umbrella, that's probability.

37
00:01:39,390 --> 00:01:41,997
When you guess on a quiz question with four choices,

38
00:01:41,997 --> 00:01:44,206
and you know you have one chance in four,

39
00:01:44,206 --> 00:01:45,620
that's probability.

40
00:01:46,070 --> 00:01:48,957
When you wonder if it's worth playing a game at the school fair,

41
00:01:48,957 --> 00:01:50,320
that's probability.

42
00:01:50,770 --> 00:01:53,060
When a doctor says a medicine works for most people,

43
00:01:53,060 --> 00:01:55,668
or a sports commentator says a team is the favourite,

44
00:01:55,668 --> 00:01:57,480
they're talking in probabilities too.

45
00:01:57,930 --> 00:02:00,792
And when someone says, I've lost five times in a row,

46
00:02:00,792 --> 00:02:04,026
so I'm due for a win, that's a probability mistake.

47
00:02:04,026 --> 00:02:05,550
We'll fix that one later.

48
00:02:06,350 --> 00:02:09,200
Probability is simply the math of not knowing for sure.

49
00:02:10,200 --> 00:02:15,107
Games, sports, quizzes, weather, even deciding whether to carry

50
00:02:15,107 --> 00:02:19,100
a jacket. They're all little bets about an uncertain future.

51
00:02:19,550 --> 00:02:21,399
You can't know what the next roll will be.

52
00:02:21,399 --> 00:02:23,971
But you can know a lot about what tends to happen.

53
00:02:23,971 --> 00:02:25,820
And that's surprisingly powerful.

54
00:02:26,270 --> 00:02:28,420
So let's build it from the very first brick.

55
00:02:29,020 --> 00:02:31,022
A probability is just a number.

56
00:02:31,022 --> 00:02:33,510
It tells you how likely something is to happen.

57
00:02:33,960 --> 00:02:36,650
And this number always lives between zero and one.

58
00:02:37,100 --> 00:02:39,820
Zero means impossible. It will never happen.

59
00:02:40,270 --> 00:02:42,886
Like rolling a seven on one ordinary die.

60
00:02:42,886 --> 00:02:46,359
A die only has the numbers one to six, so seven can never come

61
00:02:46,359 --> 00:02:46,750
up.

62
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:49,850
One means certain. It will always happen.

63
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:53,646
Like rolling a number smaller than seven on that same die.

64
00:02:53,646 --> 00:02:56,170
Every face works, so it's guaranteed.

65
00:02:56,620 --> 00:02:59,582
And everything else, everything that might or might not happen,

66
00:02:59,582 --> 00:03:00,840
sits somewhere in between.

67
00:03:01,440 --> 00:03:04,710
Right in the middle, at one half, is a perfect toss-up.

68
00:03:05,160 --> 00:03:07,360
Like flipping a coin and getting heads.

69
00:03:07,360 --> 00:03:09,560
Half the time yes, half the time no.

70
00:03:10,010 --> 00:03:13,657
The closer a number is to one, the more likely it is.

71
00:03:13,657 --> 00:03:16,550
The closer it is to zero, the less likely.

72
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:20,646
And a probability can never be negative, and never bigger than

73
00:03:20,646 --> 00:03:23,655
one. If you ever get an answer like one point two,

74
00:03:23,655 --> 00:03:26,150
or minus a half, you know something went wrong.

75
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:29,228
Let's place a few everyday things on this line,

76
00:03:29,228 --> 00:03:30,050
just by feel.

77
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:32,021
The sun rising tomorrow?

78
00:03:32,021 --> 00:03:33,170
Right up near one.

79
00:03:33,620 --> 00:03:36,821
Rain tomorrow, when the forecast says seventy percent?

80
00:03:36,821 --> 00:03:38,330
Around zero point seven.

81
00:03:38,780 --> 00:03:41,331
Guessing a quiz question right with four choices?

82
00:03:41,331 --> 00:03:42,610
Zero point two five.

83
00:03:43,060 --> 00:03:47,060
Winning a big lottery? So close to zero that you can't even see

84
00:03:47,060 --> 00:03:47,700
the gap.

85
00:03:48,500 --> 00:03:51,384
Now, there are three ways to write the same probability,

86
00:03:51,384 --> 00:03:53,070
and you'll see all three everywhere.

87
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:55,120
As a fraction, like one half.

88
00:03:55,570 --> 00:03:57,810
As a decimal, like zero point five.

89
00:03:58,260 --> 00:04:00,542
Or as a percent, like fifty percent.

90
00:04:00,542 --> 00:04:02,420
Percent just means out of a hundred.

91
00:04:02,870 --> 00:04:04,945
They are all the same amount.

92
00:04:04,945 --> 00:04:08,157
One half, zero point five, fifty percent.

93
00:04:08,157 --> 00:04:10,220
Three outfits, one person.

94
00:04:11,220 --> 00:04:14,636
To change a decimal into a percent, just multiply by one

95
00:04:14,636 --> 00:04:17,740
hundred. Zero point two five becomes twenty five percent.

96
00:04:18,190 --> 00:04:20,858
We'll mostly use fractions in this video,

97
00:04:20,858 --> 00:04:23,080
because fractions show you the counting.

98
00:04:23,530 --> 00:04:26,346
But what does a probability actually mean?

99
00:04:26,346 --> 00:04:28,762
If the chance of rain is seventy percent,

100
00:04:28,762 --> 00:04:29,980
will it rain or not?

101
00:04:30,780 --> 00:04:32,260
Here's the honest answer.

102
00:04:32,260 --> 00:04:35,180
A probability doesn't tell you what will happen next time.

103
00:04:35,630 --> 00:04:37,488
It tells you what happens in the long run.

104
00:04:37,488 --> 00:04:40,002
Out of many days with a forecast like this one,

105
00:04:40,002 --> 00:04:42,680
about seventy out of every hundred will have rain.

106
00:04:43,130 --> 00:04:45,742
So on any one day, it might stay dry.

107
00:04:45,742 --> 00:04:47,740
That doesn't mean the forecast was wrong.

108
00:04:48,190 --> 00:04:49,791
Keep that idea in your pocket.

109
00:04:49,791 --> 00:04:52,601
Probability is about patterns over many tries,

110
00:04:52,601 --> 00:04:54,030
not about one single try.

111
00:04:55,030 --> 00:04:56,987
And where do probabilities come from?

112
00:04:56,987 --> 00:04:58,250
There are three main ways.

113
00:04:58,700 --> 00:05:02,624
One, you can count. Like a die with six equal faces.

114
00:05:02,624 --> 00:05:04,010
We'll do lots of this.

115
00:05:04,460 --> 00:05:06,411
Two, you can experiment.

116
00:05:06,411 --> 00:05:10,083
Try something many times and see how often it happens.

117
00:05:10,083 --> 00:05:13,540
Like checking how often your bus is late over a whole term.

118
00:05:13,990 --> 00:05:17,496
And three, sometimes people simply give their best judgement,

119
00:05:17,496 --> 00:05:20,782
like a coach saying, I think we have a sixty percent chance of

120
00:05:20,782 --> 00:05:21,820
winning this match.

121
00:05:22,270 --> 00:05:24,066
That last kind is the shakiest.

122
00:05:24,066 --> 00:05:26,758
In this video, we'll stick to the first two,

123
00:05:26,758 --> 00:05:29,230
where we can actually check our answers.

124
00:05:30,030 --> 00:05:32,425
So how do we actually find a probability?

125
00:05:32,425 --> 00:05:34,730
For lots of everyday questions, you just count.

126
00:05:35,180 --> 00:05:36,470
Let's start with one die.

127
00:05:36,920 --> 00:05:40,594
When you roll it, there are six things that can happen.

128
00:05:40,594 --> 00:05:44,182
It can land on one, two, three, four, five,

129
00:05:44,182 --> 00:05:44,900
or six.

130
00:05:45,350 --> 00:05:47,640
Each of these is called an outcome.

131
00:05:47,640 --> 00:05:50,160
An outcome is one possible result.

132
00:05:50,610 --> 00:05:53,378
And the list of all possible outcomes has a name too.

133
00:05:53,378 --> 00:05:54,690
It's called the sample space.

134
00:05:54,690 --> 00:05:57,640
That's just a fancy name for everything that could happen.

135
00:05:58,440 --> 00:06:02,058
Now, if the die is fair, each face is equally likely.

136
00:06:02,058 --> 00:06:04,470
No face is special. The die isn't loaded.

137
00:06:04,920 --> 00:06:06,940
So what's the chance of rolling a four?

138
00:06:07,390 --> 00:06:10,088
There is one outcome we want, the four.

139
00:06:10,088 --> 00:06:12,340
Out of six outcomes in total.

140
00:06:12,340 --> 00:06:14,593
So the chance is one out of six.

141
00:06:14,593 --> 00:06:15,490
One sixth.

142
00:06:16,490 --> 00:06:17,601
That's the whole recipe.

143
00:06:17,601 --> 00:06:20,673
Probability equals the number of outcomes you want,

144
00:06:20,673 --> 00:06:23,210
divided by the total number of outcomes.

145
00:06:24,410 --> 00:06:27,328
The letter P is just short for probability.

146
00:06:27,328 --> 00:06:31,712
When you see P of something, read it as, the probability of that

147
00:06:31,712 --> 00:06:32,140
thing.

148
00:06:32,590 --> 00:06:35,486
Let's try another one. What's the chance of rolling an even

149
00:06:35,486 --> 00:06:36,040
number?

150
00:06:36,490 --> 00:06:39,069
The even numbers are two, four and six.

151
00:06:39,069 --> 00:06:40,650
That's three outcomes we want.

152
00:06:41,100 --> 00:06:44,140
Three out of six. Which is the same as one half.

153
00:06:44,940 --> 00:06:49,160
A group of outcomes we care about, like rolling an even number,

154
00:06:49,160 --> 00:06:50,640
is called an event.

155
00:06:51,090 --> 00:06:53,816
What about rolling a number bigger than four?

156
00:06:53,816 --> 00:06:57,190
That's five or six. Two out of six, which is one third.

157
00:06:57,640 --> 00:06:59,154
See how simple this is?

158
00:06:59,154 --> 00:07:01,923
Count what you want. Count everything.

159
00:07:01,923 --> 00:07:02,660
Divide.

160
00:07:03,660 --> 00:07:06,500
But there's one small rule hiding inside this recipe,

161
00:07:06,500 --> 00:07:08,030
and it's really important.

162
00:07:08,480 --> 00:07:11,353
The counting trick only works when every outcome is equally

163
00:07:11,353 --> 00:07:12,080
likely.

164
00:07:12,880 --> 00:07:14,884
Here's an example where it breaks.

165
00:07:14,884 --> 00:07:18,451
Imagine a spinner where the red part is three quarters of the

166
00:07:18,451 --> 00:07:21,120
circle, and the blue part is one quarter.

167
00:07:21,570 --> 00:07:25,656
There are two colours. But the chance of red is not one half.

168
00:07:25,656 --> 00:07:28,830
It's three quarters, because red takes up more space.

169
00:07:29,280 --> 00:07:32,783
Fix it by cutting the circle into equal pieces first.

170
00:07:32,783 --> 00:07:35,803
Four equal quarters. Three of them are red.

171
00:07:35,803 --> 00:07:37,430
Now counting works again.

172
00:07:38,430 --> 00:07:41,639
So, always ask, are my outcomes equally likely?

173
00:07:41,639 --> 00:07:44,150
If they aren't, split them into pieces that are.

174
00:07:44,600 --> 00:07:47,010
Now let's level up, and flip two coins.

175
00:07:47,460 --> 00:07:49,890
What's the chance you get one head and one tail?

176
00:07:50,340 --> 00:07:52,824
Many people think there are three outcomes.

177
00:07:52,824 --> 00:07:55,337
Two heads, two tails, or one of each.

178
00:07:55,337 --> 00:07:57,410
So the answer must be one third.

179
00:07:57,860 --> 00:08:00,280
But those three aren't equally likely.

180
00:08:00,280 --> 00:08:00,700
Watch.

181
00:08:01,150 --> 00:08:05,213
Let's draw a tree. The first coin can be heads or tails.

182
00:08:05,213 --> 00:08:09,880
Then for each of those, the second coin can be heads or tails.

183
00:08:10,330 --> 00:08:14,185
That gives four outcomes.

184
00:08:14,185 --> 00:08:16,703
Heads heads. Heads tails.

185
00:08:16,703 --> 00:08:19,220
Tails heads. Tails tails.

186
00:08:19,670 --> 00:08:21,895
And these four are equally likely.

187
00:08:21,895 --> 00:08:24,120
Each one has a chance of one quarter.

188
00:08:24,570 --> 00:08:29,774
Now count. One head and one tail happens two ways,

189
00:08:29,774 --> 00:08:32,780
heads then tails, or tails then heads.

190
00:08:33,230 --> 00:08:35,255
Two out of four. One half.

191
00:08:35,255 --> 00:08:36,380
Not one third.

192
00:08:37,580 --> 00:08:39,996
The lesson? Keep track of which coin is which.

193
00:08:39,996 --> 00:08:42,784
Heads tails and tails heads are different outcomes,

194
00:08:42,784 --> 00:08:44,980
even if they look the same at the end.

195
00:08:45,430 --> 00:08:47,434
Let's push it one step further.

196
00:08:47,434 --> 00:08:48,100
Three coins.

197
00:08:48,550 --> 00:08:51,060
Each new coin doubles the branches.

198
00:08:51,060 --> 00:08:54,010
Two, then four, then eight outcomes.

199
00:08:54,460 --> 00:08:56,780
What's the chance of getting exactly two heads?

200
00:08:57,230 --> 00:09:01,435
Look through the eight leaves.

201
00:09:01,435 --> 00:09:05,859
Heads heads tails. Heads tails heads.

202
00:09:05,859 --> 00:09:09,620
Tails heads heads. That's three.

203
00:09:10,070 --> 00:09:12,680
So the answer is three out of eight.

204
00:09:13,680 --> 00:09:15,435
A tree is a wonderful tool.

205
00:09:15,435 --> 00:09:18,151
When you're not sure how many outcomes there are,

206
00:09:18,151 --> 00:09:20,510
draw the tree, and the leaves will tell you.

207
00:09:20,960 --> 00:09:23,787
Trees are great, but they can get huge very quickly.

208
00:09:23,787 --> 00:09:26,211
So here's a shortcut for counting outcomes,

209
00:09:26,211 --> 00:09:27,900
without drawing every branch.

210
00:09:28,350 --> 00:09:31,192
Imagine you have three shirts and four pairs of trousers.

211
00:09:31,192 --> 00:09:33,630
How many different outfits can you make?

212
00:09:34,080 --> 00:09:37,637
Put the shirts down the side and the trousers along the top,

213
00:09:37,637 --> 00:09:40,680
and you get a grid. Three rows, four columns.

214
00:09:41,130 --> 00:09:43,550
Twelve squares. Twelve outfits.

215
00:09:43,550 --> 00:09:45,090
Three times four.

216
00:09:46,090 --> 00:09:49,308
This is called the multiplication rule for counting.

217
00:09:49,308 --> 00:09:52,312
If one choice can be made in some number of ways,

218
00:09:52,312 --> 00:09:54,887
and a second choice in some number of ways,

219
00:09:54,887 --> 00:09:57,040
then together, you multiply.

220
00:09:57,490 --> 00:10:00,927
Add two pairs of shoes, and every one of those twelve outfits

221
00:10:00,927 --> 00:10:03,780
splits into two. Twelve times two is twenty four.

222
00:10:04,230 --> 00:10:07,569
So if you get dressed in the dark, and grab each item at random,

223
00:10:07,569 --> 00:10:10,979
the chance of getting one particular outfit is one out of twenty

224
00:10:10,979 --> 00:10:11,370
four.

225
00:10:12,370 --> 00:10:16,020
Here's another one. A phone lock with a four digit code.

226
00:10:16,020 --> 00:10:18,848
Each digit can be anything from zero to nine.

227
00:10:18,848 --> 00:10:19,870
That's ten choices.

228
00:10:20,320 --> 00:10:25,160
First digit, ten ways. Second digit, ten ways.

229
00:10:25,160 --> 00:10:27,480
Third, ten. Fourth, ten.

230
00:10:27,930 --> 00:10:30,650
Ten times ten times ten times ten.

231
00:10:30,650 --> 00:10:32,620
Ten thousand possible codes.

232
00:10:33,620 --> 00:10:36,450
So if a stranger picks up your phone and guesses once,

233
00:10:36,450 --> 00:10:39,480
their chance of getting it right is one in ten thousand.

234
00:10:39,930 --> 00:10:43,170
And with three different guesses, it's three in ten thousand.

235
00:10:43,170 --> 00:10:43,990
Still tiny.

236
00:10:44,440 --> 00:10:47,433
That's why phones lock you out after a few wrong tries.

237
00:10:47,433 --> 00:10:50,427
They keep the number of guesses small, so the chance stays

238
00:10:50,427 --> 00:10:50,860
small.

239
00:10:51,860 --> 00:10:55,249
And this is exactly why two dice give thirty six outcomes.

240
00:10:55,249 --> 00:10:58,660
Six ways for the orange die, times six ways for the blue die.

241
00:10:59,110 --> 00:11:03,395
Count the choices, multiply, and you know the size of your

242
00:11:03,395 --> 00:11:03,890
world.

243
00:11:04,690 --> 00:11:07,083
The multiplication rule has another trick.

244
00:11:07,083 --> 00:11:10,200
It can count how many ways there are to put things in order.

245
00:11:10,650 --> 00:11:14,120
Say you have three books, A, B and C, and you put them on a

246
00:11:14,120 --> 00:11:15,651
shelf in a random order.

247
00:11:15,651 --> 00:11:17,370
How many orders are there?

248
00:11:17,820 --> 00:11:20,484
For the first spot, you have three books to choose from.

249
00:11:20,484 --> 00:11:24,158
Once one is placed, two books are left for the second spot.

250
00:11:24,158 --> 00:11:26,400
And then just one book for the last spot.

251
00:11:26,850 --> 00:11:28,466
Three times two times one.

252
00:11:28,466 --> 00:11:29,210
Six orders.

253
00:11:30,210 --> 00:11:33,046
So the chance they land in alphabetical order,

254
00:11:33,046 --> 00:11:35,700
A then B then C, is one out of six.

255
00:11:36,150 --> 00:11:39,404
Now try ten books. Ten times nine times eight,

256
00:11:39,404 --> 00:11:41,416
all the way down to one.

257
00:11:41,416 --> 00:11:44,450
That's over three and a half million orders.

258
00:11:44,900 --> 00:11:47,677
The chance that ten books shuffled at random land in perfect

259
00:11:47,677 --> 00:11:50,673
order is about one in three and a half million.

260
00:11:50,673 --> 00:11:51,290
Tiny.

261
00:11:52,290 --> 00:11:55,314
That's why a well shuffled deck of cards is almost certainly in

262
00:11:55,314 --> 00:11:57,801
an order that has never existed before.

263
00:11:57,801 --> 00:12:00,100
There are just too many possible orders.

264
00:12:00,550 --> 00:12:05,301
Now, a lottery. Suppose you must pick six numbers out of forty

265
00:12:05,301 --> 00:12:07,770
nine, and the order doesn't matter.

266
00:12:08,220 --> 00:12:12,228
If order mattered, there would be forty nine times forty eight

267
00:12:12,228 --> 00:12:16,684
times forty seven times forty six times forty five times forty

268
00:12:16,684 --> 00:12:17,350
four ways.

269
00:12:17,800 --> 00:12:21,216
But the same six numbers can be picked in any order.

270
00:12:21,216 --> 00:12:25,058
Six times five times four times three times two times one,

271
00:12:25,058 --> 00:12:27,622
which is seven hundred and twenty orders.

272
00:12:27,622 --> 00:12:29,120
So we divide by that.

273
00:12:29,570 --> 00:12:32,090
The result is almost fourteen million different tickets.

274
00:12:33,090 --> 00:12:36,930
So the chance one ticket wins is about one in fourteen million.

275
00:12:36,930 --> 00:12:39,764
Remember that tiny gap near zero on our line?

276
00:12:39,764 --> 00:12:40,990
That's what it looks like.

277
00:12:41,990 --> 00:12:43,702
Here's a way to feel that number.

278
00:12:43,702 --> 00:12:47,072
If you bought one ticket every week, you'd expect to wait over a

279
00:12:47,072 --> 00:12:48,950
quarter of a million years for a win.

280
00:12:49,400 --> 00:12:52,320
Counting carefully turns a vague feeling,

281
00:12:52,320 --> 00:12:54,970
maybe I'll win, into a clear number.

282
00:12:55,770 --> 00:12:59,090
Now we have enough tools to solve our game night puzzle.

283
00:12:59,540 --> 00:13:00,833
We're rolling two dice.

284
00:13:00,833 --> 00:13:04,490
To keep them apart, let's make one orange and one blue.

285
00:13:04,940 --> 00:13:06,560
The orange die can land six ways.

286
00:13:06,560 --> 00:13:09,100
And for each of those, the blue die can land six ways.

287
00:13:09,550 --> 00:13:12,227
So let's draw a grid. Orange along the bottom,

288
00:13:12,227 --> 00:13:15,090
blue up the side. Every square is one outcome.

289
00:13:15,540 --> 00:13:19,020
This square, for example, is orange three and blue five.

290
00:13:19,470 --> 00:13:25,820
Six columns, six rows. Six times six is thirty six outcomes.

291
00:13:26,620 --> 00:13:29,431
And just like with the coins, orange three blue five is

292
00:13:29,431 --> 00:13:31,594
different from orange five blue three.

293
00:13:31,594 --> 00:13:33,110
Two different squares.

294
00:13:33,560 --> 00:13:37,516
With fair dice, all thirty six squares are equally likely.

295
00:13:37,516 --> 00:13:38,770
So now we can count.

296
00:13:39,220 --> 00:13:42,810
Let's write the total of the two dice inside every square.

297
00:13:43,610 --> 00:13:44,873
Do you see the pattern?

298
00:13:44,873 --> 00:13:47,180
Equal totals line up along slanted lines.

299
00:13:47,630 --> 00:13:50,360
Now, how many squares have a total of twelve?

300
00:13:50,810 --> 00:13:53,803
Only one. Orange six and blue six.

301
00:13:53,803 --> 00:13:55,300
The top right corner.

302
00:13:55,750 --> 00:13:58,450
So the chance of rolling twelve is one out of thirty six.

303
00:13:59,450 --> 00:14:01,250
And a total of seven?

304
00:14:01,700 --> 00:14:04,166
Count them. One and six.

305
00:14:04,166 --> 00:14:06,633
Two and five. Three and four.

306
00:14:06,633 --> 00:14:09,099
Four and three. Five and two.

307
00:14:09,099 --> 00:14:10,670
Six and one.

308
00:14:11,120 --> 00:14:14,221
Six squares. Six out of thirty six.

309
00:14:14,221 --> 00:14:15,920
Which is one sixth.

310
00:14:17,120 --> 00:14:19,961
There it is. Seven has six ways to happen.

311
00:14:19,961 --> 00:14:24,330
Twelve has only one. That's why seven is six times more likely.

312
00:14:25,830 --> 00:14:29,401
The dice aren't magic. Seven is simply the total with the most

313
00:14:29,401 --> 00:14:30,410
ways to be made.

314
00:14:30,860 --> 00:14:33,880
Let's count the ways for every total, and build a chart.

315
00:14:34,330 --> 00:14:37,819
Two can only be made one way.

316
00:14:37,819 --> 00:14:40,878
Three, two ways. Four, three ways.

317
00:14:40,878 --> 00:14:43,937
Five, four ways. Six, five ways.

318
00:14:43,937 --> 00:14:45,830
Seven, six ways.

319
00:14:46,280 --> 00:14:48,597
And then it comes back down.

320
00:14:48,597 --> 00:14:50,975
Eight, five ways. Nine, four.

321
00:14:50,975 --> 00:14:53,652
Ten, three. Eleven, two.

322
00:14:53,652 --> 00:14:55,590
And twelve, just one.

323
00:14:56,040 --> 00:14:59,060
A perfect little mountain, with seven at the peak.

324
00:15:00,060 --> 00:15:03,045
Add up all the bars, and you get thirty six.

325
00:15:03,045 --> 00:15:05,810
Every square is counted exactly once.

326
00:15:06,260 --> 00:15:09,660
If we divide every bar by thirty six, each bar becomes a

327
00:15:09,660 --> 00:15:12,043
probability. This picture has a name.

328
00:15:12,043 --> 00:15:14,560
It's called a probability distribution.

329
00:15:15,360 --> 00:15:18,640
A distribution is just a map that shows how the chances are

330
00:15:18,640 --> 00:15:20,790
spread out over all the possible results.

331
00:15:21,240 --> 00:15:24,305
Now you can answer lots of questions just by reading the chart.

332
00:15:24,305 --> 00:15:26,042
The chance of a total of eight?

333
00:15:26,042 --> 00:15:27,400
Five out of thirty six.

334
00:15:27,850 --> 00:15:30,031
The chance of a total of two or twelve?

335
00:15:30,031 --> 00:15:32,211
Only one out of thirty six each.

336
00:15:32,211 --> 00:15:34,000
They're the rarest rolls in the game.

337
00:15:34,450 --> 00:15:37,782
Here's another one. What's the chance of rolling a double,

338
00:15:37,782 --> 00:15:39,544
both dice showing the same number?

339
00:15:39,544 --> 00:15:42,463
That's the diagonal from the bottom left to the top right.

340
00:15:42,463 --> 00:15:44,060
Six squares. One sixth.

341
00:15:44,510 --> 00:15:47,539
And the chance of a total of four or less?

342
00:15:47,539 --> 00:15:51,007
Two has one way, three has two, four has three.

343
00:15:51,007 --> 00:15:53,490
Six squares again. One sixth.

344
00:15:53,940 --> 00:15:57,076
Many board games are designed around this exact mountain,

345
00:15:57,076 --> 00:15:58,840
even if the players never notice.

346
00:15:59,840 --> 00:16:03,574
But hold on. Does real life actually follow this counting?

347
00:16:03,574 --> 00:16:04,500
Let's test it.

348
00:16:04,950 --> 00:16:07,820
Imagine flipping a fair coin ten times.

349
00:16:08,270 --> 00:16:10,118
The chance of heads is one half.

350
00:16:10,118 --> 00:16:12,169
So should you get exactly five heads?

351
00:16:12,169 --> 00:16:13,610
Not necessarily.

352
00:16:14,060 --> 00:16:16,672
In this run we got seven heads out of ten.

353
00:16:16,672 --> 00:16:19,070
That's seventy percent, not fifty.

354
00:16:19,520 --> 00:16:21,295
Is the coin broken? No.

355
00:16:21,295 --> 00:16:24,850
With only ten flips, wobbles like this happen all the time.

356
00:16:25,650 --> 00:16:28,088
Now let's flip it a hundred times.

357
00:16:28,088 --> 00:16:30,500
Then a thousand. Then ten thousand.

358
00:16:30,950 --> 00:16:32,443
Watch the share of heads.

359
00:16:32,443 --> 00:16:34,700
At first it jumps around wildly.

360
00:16:35,150 --> 00:16:37,396
But the more flips we do, the more it settles down,

361
00:16:37,396 --> 00:16:38,970
closer and closer to one half.

362
00:16:40,170 --> 00:16:42,084
This is called the law of large numbers.

363
00:16:42,084 --> 00:16:45,573
Over many, many tries, what actually happens gets closer to the

364
00:16:45,573 --> 00:16:46,640
probability.

365
00:16:47,640 --> 00:16:49,267
Let's do the same with our two dice.

366
00:16:49,267 --> 00:16:51,948
We'll roll them thirty six times, and build a chart of the

367
00:16:51,948 --> 00:16:52,520
totals.

368
00:16:52,970 --> 00:16:56,687
After thirty six rolls, the chart is bumpy and messy.

369
00:16:56,687 --> 00:16:58,780
Some totals didn't even show up.

370
00:16:59,230 --> 00:17:02,990
After three hundred and sixty rolls, a shape starts to appear.

371
00:17:03,440 --> 00:17:06,785
And after thirty six thousand rolls, look.

372
00:17:06,785 --> 00:17:09,840
It's our mountain. Almost exactly.

373
00:17:11,340 --> 00:17:14,287
The counting we did on paper predicted what happens when you

374
00:17:14,287 --> 00:17:17,490
really roll the dice. That's the power of probability.

375
00:17:17,940 --> 00:17:21,232
This also gives us a second way to find a probability.

376
00:17:21,232 --> 00:17:24,183
When you can't count the outcomes, you can try something many

377
00:17:24,183 --> 00:17:26,330
times, and see how often it happens.

378
00:17:26,780 --> 00:17:28,752
You can try this yourself tonight.

379
00:17:28,752 --> 00:17:32,696
Flip a coin twenty times, and write down how many heads you get.

380
00:17:32,696 --> 00:17:34,669
Then ask a friend to do the same.

381
00:17:34,669 --> 00:17:38,612
Your numbers will probably be different, and that's completely

382
00:17:38,612 --> 00:17:39,270
normal.

383
00:17:39,720 --> 00:17:42,933
That's how a weather forecast works, in a simple sense.

384
00:17:42,933 --> 00:17:45,713
Out of many past days that looked like today,

385
00:17:45,713 --> 00:17:47,000
how many had rain?

386
00:17:47,450 --> 00:17:50,013
But remember the pocket idea.

387
00:17:50,013 --> 00:17:54,879
Many tries, not one. The law of large numbers says nothing about

388
00:17:54,879 --> 00:17:56,140
the next single flip.

389
00:17:57,140 --> 00:18:00,840
In fact, small numbers of tries can look surprisingly streaky.

390
00:18:01,290 --> 00:18:04,797
If you flip a coin twenty times, getting four or more heads in a

391
00:18:04,797 --> 00:18:06,757
row somewhere is not rare at all.

392
00:18:06,757 --> 00:18:08,910
It happens in nearly half of all runs.

393
00:18:09,360 --> 00:18:11,724
Random doesn't mean nicely mixed.

394
00:18:11,724 --> 00:18:13,611
Random means unpredictable.

395
00:18:13,611 --> 00:18:16,690
And unpredictable things are often clumpy.

396
00:18:17,690 --> 00:18:20,584
Here's a fun way to use what we just learned.

397
00:18:20,584 --> 00:18:23,493
Suppose a friend hands you a die, and you wonder,

398
00:18:23,493 --> 00:18:24,330
is it fair?

399
00:18:24,780 --> 00:18:28,329
If it's fair, each face should come up one sixth of the time.

400
00:18:28,329 --> 00:18:32,100
So in sixty rolls, you'd expect each face about ten times.

401
00:18:32,550 --> 00:18:34,909
Let's roll it sixty times.

402
00:18:34,909 --> 00:18:38,929
We get eight ones, eleven twos, nine threes,

403
00:18:38,929 --> 00:18:42,730
twelve fours, seven fives and thirteen sixes.

404
00:18:43,180 --> 00:18:46,599
Not exactly ten each. But is that suspicious?

405
00:18:46,599 --> 00:18:51,011
No. This kind of wobble is completely normal for a fair die,

406
00:18:51,011 --> 00:18:52,720
with only sixty rolls.

407
00:18:53,720 --> 00:18:57,023
Now a different die. Sixty rolls, and twenty five of them are

408
00:18:57,023 --> 00:18:57,580
sixes.

409
00:18:58,030 --> 00:18:59,183
That's way more than ten.

410
00:18:59,183 --> 00:19:02,795
For a fair die, a result this extreme would be extremely rare.

411
00:19:02,795 --> 00:19:05,440
So you'd have good reason to think this die is loaded.

412
00:19:06,440 --> 00:19:10,091
Notice what we did. We used probability to predict what fair

413
00:19:10,091 --> 00:19:13,200
looks like, then compared it with what really happened.

414
00:19:13,650 --> 00:19:15,814
Small differences, we shrug off.

415
00:19:15,814 --> 00:19:18,188
Huge differences make us suspicious.

416
00:19:18,188 --> 00:19:21,645
Scientists use this same idea, with more careful math,

417
00:19:21,645 --> 00:19:24,450
to test whether something is really going on.

418
00:19:25,250 --> 00:19:27,810
Let's take a breath, and look at how far we've come.

419
00:19:28,260 --> 00:19:31,668
A probability is a number from zero to one.

420
00:19:31,668 --> 00:19:34,300
Zero is impossible, one is certain.

421
00:19:34,750 --> 00:19:38,217
When outcomes are equally likely, the probability is the

422
00:19:38,217 --> 00:19:40,930
outcomes you want, divided by all the outcomes.

423
00:19:41,380 --> 00:19:44,550
To find all the outcomes, draw a tree or a grid,

424
00:19:44,550 --> 00:19:46,870
or multiply the number of choices.

425
00:19:47,320 --> 00:19:50,230
With two dice, seven is the most likely total,

426
00:19:50,230 --> 00:19:52,350
because it has the most ways to be made.

427
00:19:52,800 --> 00:19:55,456
And over many tries, real results settle close to the

428
00:19:55,456 --> 00:19:58,520
probability. But a single try can still surprise you.

429
00:19:59,520 --> 00:20:03,023
If all of that makes sense, you've already got the foundation.

430
00:20:03,023 --> 00:20:05,540
Everything from here is built on top of it.

431
00:20:06,340 --> 00:20:09,156
Now for the three little words that unlock almost every

432
00:20:09,156 --> 00:20:12,210
probability question. The first one is not.

433
00:20:12,660 --> 00:20:15,375
Something either happens, or it doesn't.

434
00:20:15,375 --> 00:20:16,960
There's no third option.

435
00:20:17,410 --> 00:20:20,275
So the chance it happens, plus the chance it doesn't,

436
00:20:20,275 --> 00:20:23,140
must add up to one. To everything.

437
00:20:23,590 --> 00:20:27,100
That means the chance something does not happen is one,

438
00:20:27,100 --> 00:20:28,730
minus the chance it does.

439
00:20:29,730 --> 00:20:32,299
A here is just a name for any event.

440
00:20:32,299 --> 00:20:34,020
Like, rolling a seven.

441
00:20:34,470 --> 00:20:38,426
Let's try it. The chance of rolling a total of seven is one

442
00:20:38,426 --> 00:20:42,381
sixth. So the chance of not rolling seven is one minus one

443
00:20:42,381 --> 00:20:43,920
sixth, which is five sixths.

444
00:20:44,370 --> 00:20:46,089
And you can check it on the grid.

445
00:20:46,089 --> 00:20:47,995
Thirty squares are not seven.

446
00:20:47,995 --> 00:20:50,088
Thirty out of thirty six is five sixths.

447
00:20:50,088 --> 00:20:50,870
It matches.

448
00:20:51,870 --> 00:20:53,135
Here's why this matters.

449
00:20:53,135 --> 00:20:55,446
Sometimes the thing you want is hard to count,

450
00:20:55,446 --> 00:20:57,060
but the opposite is easy.

451
00:20:57,510 --> 00:21:00,814
For example, what's the chance of rolling a total that is not

452
00:21:00,814 --> 00:21:01,240
twelve?

453
00:21:01,690 --> 00:21:04,022
You could count thirty five squares.

454
00:21:04,022 --> 00:21:07,101
Or you can just say, twelve has one square,

455
00:21:07,101 --> 00:21:11,280
so not twelve is one minus one over thirty six,

456
00:21:11,280 --> 00:21:14,140
which is thirty five over thirty six.

457
00:21:14,590 --> 00:21:16,854
When counting the thing you want looks painful,

458
00:21:16,854 --> 00:21:20,990
flip it around. Count the opposite, and take it away from one.

459
00:21:21,990 --> 00:21:25,570
This little trick becomes a superpower in a few minutes.

460
00:21:25,570 --> 00:21:26,580
Hold on to it.

461
00:21:27,030 --> 00:21:30,471
While we're here, let's clear up a word that confuses a lot of

462
00:21:30,471 --> 00:21:31,340
people. Odds.

463
00:21:31,790 --> 00:21:34,492
People often say odds when they mean probability.

464
00:21:34,492 --> 00:21:36,430
But they are not quite the same thing.

465
00:21:36,880 --> 00:21:40,930
Probability compares the ways something happens to all the ways.

466
00:21:40,930 --> 00:21:43,770
Odds compare the ways it happens to the ways it doesn't.

467
00:21:44,220 --> 00:21:46,711
Take a total of seven on two dice.

468
00:21:46,711 --> 00:21:48,294
Six squares make seven.

469
00:21:48,294 --> 00:21:49,650
Thirty squares don't.

470
00:21:50,100 --> 00:21:52,546
The probability is six out of thirty six.

471
00:21:52,546 --> 00:21:53,280
One sixth.

472
00:21:53,730 --> 00:21:55,541
But the odds are six to thirty.

473
00:21:55,541 --> 00:21:57,352
Which simplifies to one to five.

474
00:21:57,352 --> 00:22:00,620
For every one way to win, there are five ways to lose.

475
00:22:01,620 --> 00:22:05,631
Notice that one to five and one sixth describe the same chance.

476
00:22:05,631 --> 00:22:08,440
One plus five makes six, the total.

477
00:22:08,890 --> 00:22:11,218
A fair coin has odds of one to one.

478
00:22:11,218 --> 00:22:13,575
One way to win, one way to lose.

479
00:22:13,575 --> 00:22:15,520
People sometimes call that evens.

480
00:22:15,970 --> 00:22:19,865
To turn odds into a probability, put the first number on top,

481
00:22:19,865 --> 00:22:22,580
and both numbers added together on the bottom.

482
00:22:23,030 --> 00:22:26,356
So odds of three to one against something mean one way it

483
00:22:26,356 --> 00:22:27,970
happens, three ways it doesn't.

484
00:22:27,970 --> 00:22:31,540
One out of four. A probability of one quarter.

485
00:22:32,540 --> 00:22:36,434
When you hear odds, just ask, is this comparing to the whole,

486
00:22:36,434 --> 00:22:38,040
or to the other side?

487
00:22:38,840 --> 00:22:42,507
The second word is or. What's the chance that this happens,

488
00:22:42,507 --> 00:22:43,720
or that happens?

489
00:22:44,170 --> 00:22:47,090
Let's start easy. On our two dice, what's the chance of rolling

490
00:22:47,090 --> 00:22:50,010
a total of seven, or a total of eleven?

491
00:22:50,460 --> 00:22:54,052
Seven has six squares. Eleven has two squares,

492
00:22:54,052 --> 00:22:56,190
five and six, and six and five.

493
00:22:56,640 --> 00:22:58,093
They don't share any squares.

494
00:22:58,093 --> 00:23:00,780
A roll can't be seven and eleven at the same time.

495
00:23:01,230 --> 00:23:06,183
So we just add them. Six plus two is eight squares.

496
00:23:06,183 --> 00:23:08,660
Eight out of thirty six.

497
00:23:09,660 --> 00:23:13,134
When two events can't happen together, the chance of one or the

498
00:23:13,134 --> 00:23:15,460
other is just the two chances added up.

499
00:23:15,910 --> 00:23:18,940
But careful. This is where a lot of people slip.

500
00:23:19,390 --> 00:23:22,348
What's the chance that the orange die shows six,

501
00:23:22,348 --> 00:23:24,210
or the blue die shows six?

502
00:23:24,660 --> 00:23:26,742
The orange six is a whole column.

503
00:23:26,742 --> 00:23:27,790
That's six squares.

504
00:23:28,240 --> 00:23:29,878
The blue six is a whole row.

505
00:23:29,878 --> 00:23:31,110
Also six squares.

506
00:23:31,560 --> 00:23:34,167
So, six plus six is twelve?

507
00:23:34,167 --> 00:23:35,110
Let's look.

508
00:23:35,560 --> 00:23:38,363
The column and the row cross in one corner,

509
00:23:38,363 --> 00:23:41,390
double six. If we add them, we count that square twice.

510
00:23:42,190 --> 00:23:44,113
So we take it away once.

511
00:23:44,113 --> 00:23:47,450
Six plus six, minus one, is eleven.

512
00:23:47,900 --> 00:23:50,060
The answer is eleven out of thirty six.

513
00:23:51,260 --> 00:23:52,843
This is the full or rule.

514
00:23:52,843 --> 00:23:56,235
Add the two chances, then take away the overlap,

515
00:23:56,235 --> 00:23:59,400
the part where both happen, so it's not counted twice.

516
00:24:00,400 --> 00:24:03,697
A picture helps. Draw two circles that overlap.

517
00:24:03,697 --> 00:24:06,773
To find the area covered by either circle,

518
00:24:06,773 --> 00:24:09,594
you add both circles, then remove the middle,

519
00:24:09,594 --> 00:24:11,480
because you counted it twice.

520
00:24:11,930 --> 00:24:15,344
If the circles don't overlap at all, the middle is zero,

521
00:24:15,344 --> 00:24:17,150
and you're back to simply adding.

522
00:24:17,600 --> 00:24:19,741
Here's an everyday example.

523
00:24:19,741 --> 00:24:22,742
In a class of thirty students, twelve play football,

524
00:24:22,742 --> 00:24:24,890
eight play music, and five do both.

525
00:24:25,340 --> 00:24:27,656
How many do football or music?

526
00:24:27,656 --> 00:24:31,136
Twelve plus eight is twenty, minus the five who were counted

527
00:24:31,136 --> 00:24:32,740
twice. Fifteen students.

528
00:24:33,190 --> 00:24:36,511
So if you pick a student at random, the chance they do football

529
00:24:36,511 --> 00:24:38,669
or music is fifteen out of thirty.

530
00:24:38,669 --> 00:24:39,470
One half.

531
00:24:40,470 --> 00:24:44,339
Whenever you hear the word or, ask yourself one question.

532
00:24:44,339 --> 00:24:45,931
Can both happen at once?

533
00:24:45,931 --> 00:24:48,200
If yes, remove the overlap.

534
00:24:48,650 --> 00:24:52,789
The third word is and. What's the chance that this happens,

535
00:24:52,789 --> 00:24:54,550
and that happens too?

536
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:58,263
Back to our dice. What's the chance of double six?

537
00:24:58,263 --> 00:25:02,090
The orange die shows six, and the blue die shows six.

538
00:25:02,540 --> 00:25:06,306
On the grid, that's the one square where the six column and the

539
00:25:06,306 --> 00:25:09,110
six row meet. One out of thirty six.

540
00:25:09,560 --> 00:25:11,775
But here's another way to see it.

541
00:25:11,775 --> 00:25:14,588
The orange die has a one in six chance of six.

542
00:25:14,588 --> 00:25:17,820
And then, the blue die has a one in six chance of six.

543
00:25:18,270 --> 00:25:23,971
Multiply them. One sixth times one sixth is one over thirty six.

544
00:25:23,971 --> 00:25:24,980
Same answer.

545
00:25:25,980 --> 00:25:28,592
Why multiply? Picture it as area.

546
00:25:28,592 --> 00:25:30,333
Take a square of side one.

547
00:25:30,333 --> 00:25:33,160
The orange six takes one sixth of the width.

548
00:25:33,160 --> 00:25:35,770
The blue six takes one sixth of the height.

549
00:25:36,220 --> 00:25:41,908
Where they overlap is a little box, one sixth wide and one sixth

550
00:25:41,908 --> 00:25:46,530
tall. Its area is one sixth times one sixth.

551
00:25:47,530 --> 00:25:49,642
This works for more than two things.

552
00:25:49,642 --> 00:25:51,484
Three dice all showing six?

553
00:25:51,484 --> 00:25:55,657
One sixth, times one sixth, times one sixth.

554
00:25:55,657 --> 00:25:58,310
One in two hundred and sixteen.

555
00:25:58,760 --> 00:26:01,108
So and usually means multiply.

556
00:26:01,108 --> 00:26:03,930
And multiplying fractions makes things smaller.

557
00:26:03,930 --> 00:26:08,170
Both things happening is less likely than either one alone.

558
00:26:08,620 --> 00:26:10,227
But there's a condition.

559
00:26:10,227 --> 00:26:13,198
This simple multiplying only works when the two events are

560
00:26:13,198 --> 00:26:14,210
independent.

561
00:26:15,010 --> 00:26:17,854
Independent means one result doesn't change the chances of the

562
00:26:17,854 --> 00:26:18,430
other.

563
00:26:18,880 --> 00:26:21,310
The orange die has no idea what the blue die did.

564
00:26:21,310 --> 00:26:22,720
They don't talk to each other.

565
00:26:22,720 --> 00:26:23,960
So they're independent.

566
00:26:24,410 --> 00:26:27,336
A coin and a die are independent too.

567
00:26:27,336 --> 00:26:31,164
The chance of heads and a six is one half times one sixth.

568
00:26:31,164 --> 00:26:32,060
One twelfth.

569
00:26:32,510 --> 00:26:35,084
But some events do affect each other.

570
00:26:35,084 --> 00:26:38,600
Here's a bag with three red marbles and two blue marbles.

571
00:26:39,050 --> 00:26:41,232
You pull out one marble, don't put it back,

572
00:26:41,232 --> 00:26:42,671
and then pull out another.

573
00:26:42,671 --> 00:26:44,110
What's the chance both are red?

574
00:26:44,560 --> 00:26:48,003
First pull. Three red out of five marbles.

575
00:26:48,003 --> 00:26:48,890
Three fifths.

576
00:26:49,340 --> 00:26:52,473
Now, if that first marble was red, the bag has changed.

577
00:26:52,473 --> 00:26:55,180
There are only two red left, out of four.

578
00:26:55,630 --> 00:26:57,464
So the second chance is two quarters.

579
00:26:57,464 --> 00:26:59,477
The first pull changed the second one.

580
00:26:59,477 --> 00:27:01,490
These events are not independent.

581
00:27:01,940 --> 00:27:04,467
Multiply the chances along the path.

582
00:27:04,467 --> 00:27:07,687
Three fifths times two quarters is six over twenty,

583
00:27:07,687 --> 00:27:08,830
which is three tenths.

584
00:27:09,830 --> 00:27:13,251
If you had put the first marble back, the bag would be the same

585
00:27:13,251 --> 00:27:15,748
both times. Three fifths times three fifths,

586
00:27:15,748 --> 00:27:17,340
nine over twenty five.

587
00:27:17,790 --> 00:27:21,329
That's a bit higher, because putting the red marble back gives

588
00:27:21,329 --> 00:27:23,540
red a better chance the second time.

589
00:27:23,990 --> 00:27:26,444
So, for and: multiply along the path.

590
00:27:26,444 --> 00:27:28,816
But always use the chance of the second thing,

591
00:27:28,816 --> 00:27:30,830
after the first one has happened.

592
00:27:31,830 --> 00:27:35,386
Because independence matters so much, let's practise spotting

593
00:27:35,386 --> 00:27:39,160
it. For each pair, ask, does the first result change the chances

594
00:27:39,160 --> 00:27:40,270
of the second?

595
00:27:40,720 --> 00:27:44,937
One. Flipping a coin today, and flipping it again tomorrow.

596
00:27:44,937 --> 00:27:47,470
Independent. The coin doesn't remember.

597
00:27:47,920 --> 00:27:50,984
Two. Drawing a card from a deck, keeping it,

598
00:27:50,984 --> 00:27:53,827
and drawing another. Not independent.

599
00:27:53,827 --> 00:27:55,140
The deck has changed.

600
00:27:55,590 --> 00:27:58,228
Three. Rain today, and rain tomorrow.

601
00:27:58,228 --> 00:28:02,184
Not independent. Rainy weather tends to stick around for a few

602
00:28:02,184 --> 00:28:05,920
days, so rain today makes rain tomorrow more likely.

603
00:28:06,370 --> 00:28:09,506
Four. Your birthday, and the birthday of a random stranger in

604
00:28:09,506 --> 00:28:11,563
another country. Independent.

605
00:28:11,563 --> 00:28:13,760
One tells you nothing about the other.

606
00:28:14,210 --> 00:28:17,774
Five. Whether you study tonight, and whether you pass tomorrow's

607
00:28:17,774 --> 00:28:20,362
test. Definitely not independent.

608
00:28:20,362 --> 00:28:21,949
Studying changes your chances.

609
00:28:21,949 --> 00:28:22,950
That's the whole point.

610
00:28:23,950 --> 00:28:27,605
When events are independent, multiply the plain chances.

611
00:28:27,605 --> 00:28:31,060
When they're not, use the updated chance for the second step.

612
00:28:31,860 --> 00:28:35,952
Now let's combine not and and, to solve one of the most useful

613
00:28:35,952 --> 00:28:38,450
questions in all of probability.

614
00:28:38,900 --> 00:28:40,689
You roll one die four times.

615
00:28:40,689 --> 00:28:42,870
What's the chance you get at least one six?

616
00:28:43,320 --> 00:28:47,416
At least one means one six, or two, or three,

617
00:28:47,416 --> 00:28:50,720
or four. That's a lot of cases to count.

618
00:28:51,170 --> 00:28:52,932
Here's a tempting shortcut.

619
00:28:52,932 --> 00:28:54,915
Each roll has a one sixth chance.

620
00:28:54,915 --> 00:28:56,677
Four rolls, so add them up.

621
00:28:56,677 --> 00:28:59,320
Four sixths, about sixty seven percent.

622
00:28:59,770 --> 00:29:01,518
But that can't be right.

623
00:29:01,518 --> 00:29:03,878
Watch what happens with six rolls.

624
00:29:03,878 --> 00:29:07,375
Six sixths is one. A hundred percent.

625
00:29:07,375 --> 00:29:10,040
As if six rolls guarantee a six.

626
00:29:10,490 --> 00:29:13,048
Anyone who's ever played a dice game knows you can roll six

627
00:29:13,048 --> 00:29:14,730
times and never see a six.

628
00:29:15,730 --> 00:29:18,115
The adding was wrong because these events can all happen

629
00:29:18,115 --> 00:29:21,110
together, so we're counting overlaps again and again.

630
00:29:21,560 --> 00:29:23,447
So let's use the superpower.

631
00:29:23,447 --> 00:29:26,664
Flip it around. The opposite of at least one six is,

632
00:29:26,664 --> 00:29:27,810
no sixes at all.

633
00:29:28,260 --> 00:29:31,760
The chance one roll is not a six is five sixths.

634
00:29:32,210 --> 00:29:33,835
The rolls are independent.

635
00:29:33,835 --> 00:29:36,513
So the chance that all four rolls are not six is five sixths,

636
00:29:36,513 --> 00:29:39,280
times five sixths, times five sixths, times five sixths.

637
00:29:39,730 --> 00:29:42,471
That's six hundred and twenty five out of one thousand two

638
00:29:42,471 --> 00:29:43,971
hundred and ninety six.

639
00:29:43,971 --> 00:29:45,470
About forty eight percent.

640
00:29:45,920 --> 00:29:49,085
So the chance of at least one six is one minus that.

641
00:29:49,085 --> 00:29:50,680
About fifty two percent.

642
00:29:51,880 --> 00:29:54,430
Just over a half. Not sixty seven percent.

643
00:29:54,880 --> 00:29:57,760
And with six rolls, it's about sixty seven percent.

644
00:29:57,760 --> 00:29:59,890
Likely, but nowhere near certain.

645
00:30:00,340 --> 00:30:03,058
Look at this curve. Every extra roll helps,

646
00:30:03,058 --> 00:30:05,970
but it never quite reaches one hundred percent.

647
00:30:06,970 --> 00:30:08,732
This trick works everywhere.

648
00:30:08,732 --> 00:30:12,037
The chance of at least one success, equals one,

649
00:30:12,037 --> 00:30:14,460
minus the chance of no success at all.

650
00:30:15,460 --> 00:30:18,667
For example, if you guess on three quiz questions,

651
00:30:18,667 --> 00:30:22,408
each with four choices, what's the chance you get at least one

652
00:30:22,408 --> 00:30:22,880
right?

653
00:30:23,330 --> 00:30:26,138
The chance of getting one wrong is three quarters.

654
00:30:26,138 --> 00:30:30,024
All three wrong is three quarters cubed, twenty seven over sixty

655
00:30:30,024 --> 00:30:30,460
four.

656
00:30:30,910 --> 00:30:34,559
So at least one right is thirty seven out of sixty four.

657
00:30:34,559 --> 00:30:35,850
A bit more than half.

658
00:30:36,850 --> 00:30:39,914
Whenever you see the words at least one, your brain should

659
00:30:39,914 --> 00:30:41,370
shout, flip it around.

660
00:30:41,820 --> 00:30:45,841
Let's practise our three words, not, or, and,

661
00:30:45,841 --> 00:30:50,470
on something new. A normal deck of playing cards.

662
00:30:50,920 --> 00:30:53,369
A deck has fifty two cards.

663
00:30:53,369 --> 00:30:58,070
There are four suits, hearts, diamonds, clubs and spades.

664
00:30:58,070 --> 00:31:02,650
And each suit has thirteen cards, from ace up to king.

665
00:31:03,100 --> 00:31:07,219
Shuffle well, and every card is equally likely to be on top.

666
00:31:07,219 --> 00:31:08,870
So our counting recipe works.

667
00:31:09,320 --> 00:31:11,696
What's the chance the top card is a heart?

668
00:31:11,696 --> 00:31:14,072
Thirteen hearts out of fifty two cards.

669
00:31:14,072 --> 00:31:15,490
That's one quarter.

670
00:31:16,490 --> 00:31:18,351
What's the chance it's a king?

671
00:31:18,351 --> 00:31:20,486
Four kings out of fifty two.

672
00:31:20,486 --> 00:31:21,800
One thirteenth.

673
00:31:22,250 --> 00:31:24,280
Now, the chance it's a king or a heart?

674
00:31:24,730 --> 00:31:26,967
Thirteen hearts, plus four kings.

675
00:31:26,967 --> 00:31:30,402
But one card is both, the king of hearts.

676
00:31:30,402 --> 00:31:31,820
Don't count it twice.

677
00:31:32,270 --> 00:31:35,443
Thirteen plus four, minus one, is sixteen.

678
00:31:35,443 --> 00:31:39,310
Sixteen out of fifty two, which simplifies to four thirteenths.

679
00:31:40,310 --> 00:31:41,759
And the chance it's not a heart?

680
00:31:41,759 --> 00:31:44,130
One minus one quarter. Three quarters.

681
00:31:44,580 --> 00:31:48,482
Now for and. You draw two cards, without putting the first one

682
00:31:48,482 --> 00:31:51,000
back. What's the chance both are aces?

683
00:31:51,450 --> 00:31:54,270
First card. Four aces out of fifty two.

684
00:31:54,720 --> 00:31:57,307
If that was an ace, there are now three aces left,

685
00:31:57,307 --> 00:31:58,600
out of fifty one cards.

686
00:31:59,050 --> 00:32:00,764
Multiply along the path.

687
00:32:00,764 --> 00:32:04,405
Four over fifty two, times three over fifty one.

688
00:32:04,405 --> 00:32:07,400
That's one out of two hundred and twenty one.

689
00:32:08,400 --> 00:32:09,868
Not even half a percent.

690
00:32:09,868 --> 00:32:11,870
Two aces in a row really is rare.

691
00:32:12,320 --> 00:32:14,957
Same three words, a completely different game.

692
00:32:14,957 --> 00:32:16,276
That's the beauty of it.

693
00:32:16,276 --> 00:32:18,730
Once you know the tools, they work everywhere.

694
00:32:19,530 --> 00:32:22,924
Real life is often a chain of events, where the second step

695
00:32:22,924 --> 00:32:26,750
depends on the first. A tree diagram handles that beautifully.

696
00:32:27,200 --> 00:32:30,459
Here's a story. On a typical school day in your town,

697
00:32:30,459 --> 00:32:32,370
there's a three in ten chance of rain.

698
00:32:32,820 --> 00:32:36,155
When it rains, the school bus is late half the time.

699
00:32:36,155 --> 00:32:40,280
When it's dry, the bus is late only one time in ten.

700
00:32:40,730 --> 00:32:43,071
These numbers are made up for this example,

701
00:32:43,071 --> 00:32:45,220
but the method works for any numbers.

702
00:32:45,670 --> 00:32:49,414
Question. On a random school day, what's the chance the bus is

703
00:32:49,414 --> 00:32:49,870
late?

704
00:32:50,320 --> 00:32:53,184
Let's imagine one hundred school days, and walk them through the

705
00:32:53,184 --> 00:32:53,580
tree.

706
00:32:54,030 --> 00:32:55,610
Three in ten days are rainy.

707
00:32:55,610 --> 00:32:56,850
That's thirty rainy days.

708
00:32:57,300 --> 00:32:59,878
On half of those, the bus is late.

709
00:32:59,878 --> 00:33:00,900
Fifteen days.

710
00:33:01,350 --> 00:33:04,683
Seventy days are dry. On one in ten of them,

711
00:33:04,683 --> 00:33:06,570
the bus is late. Seven days.

712
00:33:07,020 --> 00:33:09,809
So the bus is late on fifteen plus seven days.

713
00:33:09,809 --> 00:33:12,170
Twenty two days out of one hundred.

714
00:33:13,170 --> 00:33:16,607
With the tree, you can do this without imagining days.

715
00:33:16,607 --> 00:33:20,038
Multiply along each path to a late bus, then add the paths

716
00:33:20,038 --> 00:33:20,900
together.

717
00:33:21,350 --> 00:33:25,711
Multiply along a path, because both steps have to happen.

718
00:33:25,711 --> 00:33:29,507
That's and. Add across paths, because either path works.

719
00:33:29,507 --> 00:33:30,250
That's or.

720
00:33:31,450 --> 00:33:34,388
And a quick check. All four paths together,

721
00:33:34,388 --> 00:33:38,824
late and on time, rainy and dry, must add up to one hundred

722
00:33:38,824 --> 00:33:43,171
days. Fifteen, plus fifteen, plus seven, plus sixty three.

723
00:33:43,171 --> 00:33:44,860
One hundred. Good.

724
00:33:45,310 --> 00:33:49,080
So the tree is just and and or, working together.

725
00:33:49,530 --> 00:33:52,650
Now a sneaky question, which will lead us into our next big

726
00:33:52,650 --> 00:33:54,637
idea. The bus was late today.

727
00:33:54,637 --> 00:33:56,210
What's the chance it was raining?

728
00:33:57,210 --> 00:33:59,139
Look at the hundred days again.

729
00:33:59,139 --> 00:34:01,495
The bus was late on twenty two of them.

730
00:34:01,495 --> 00:34:02,570
That's our world now.

731
00:34:03,020 --> 00:34:05,150
Fifteen of those twenty two were rainy days.

732
00:34:05,600 --> 00:34:08,789
So the chance it was raining, given the bus was late,

733
00:34:08,789 --> 00:34:10,700
is fifteen out of twenty two.

734
00:34:10,700 --> 00:34:12,400
About sixty eight percent.

735
00:34:13,600 --> 00:34:17,158
Before you knew anything, rain had a thirty percent chance.

736
00:34:17,158 --> 00:34:20,080
Knowing the bus was late made rain much more likely.

737
00:34:20,530 --> 00:34:23,574
That idea, how a clue changes a probability,

738
00:34:23,574 --> 00:34:24,910
is what we'll look at next.

739
00:34:25,710 --> 00:34:28,565
So far, we've rolled the dice without knowing anything.

740
00:34:28,565 --> 00:34:30,510
But what if someone gives you a clue?

741
00:34:30,960 --> 00:34:33,512
This is called conditional probability.

742
00:34:33,512 --> 00:34:36,423
It's the chance of something, given that you already know

743
00:34:36,423 --> 00:34:37,360
something else.

744
00:34:37,810 --> 00:34:40,993
Here's the key idea, and it's beautiful.

745
00:34:40,993 --> 00:34:42,460
A clue shrinks the grid.

746
00:34:43,460 --> 00:34:47,201
Your friend rolls both dice, hides them, and peeks at the orange

747
00:34:47,201 --> 00:34:50,690
one. They tell you, the orange die is a six.

748
00:34:51,140 --> 00:34:52,910
What's the chance the total is twelve?

749
00:34:53,360 --> 00:34:56,528
Before the clue, it was one in thirty six.

750
00:34:56,528 --> 00:35:00,378
But now, every square where orange isn't six is impossible.

751
00:35:00,378 --> 00:35:01,280
Cross them out.

752
00:35:01,730 --> 00:35:03,795
We're left with just one column.

753
00:35:03,795 --> 00:35:05,850
Six squares. That's our new world.

754
00:35:06,300 --> 00:35:11,400
And in this new world, a total of twelve is one square out of

755
00:35:11,400 --> 00:35:13,060
six. One sixth.

756
00:35:14,260 --> 00:35:16,634
The clue made twelve six times more likely.

757
00:35:16,634 --> 00:35:19,400
New information changed the probability.

758
00:35:19,850 --> 00:35:22,390
We write this with a straight line.

759
00:35:22,390 --> 00:35:25,369
P of twelve, line, orange six.

760
00:35:25,369 --> 00:35:28,460
And we read that line as, given.

761
00:35:29,460 --> 00:35:31,721
So the recipe is the same as always.

762
00:35:31,721 --> 00:35:35,425
Count the outcomes you want, but only inside the shrunken world.

763
00:35:35,425 --> 00:35:37,890
Then divide by the size of that shrunken world.

764
00:35:38,340 --> 00:35:40,351
Now here's a surprising one.

765
00:35:40,351 --> 00:35:42,979
With the same clue, the orange die is a six,

766
00:35:42,979 --> 00:35:44,990
what's the chance the total is seven?

767
00:35:45,440 --> 00:35:48,562
In the six column, only one square makes seven,

768
00:35:48,562 --> 00:35:50,122
orange six and blue one.

769
00:35:50,122 --> 00:35:51,300
One out of six.

770
00:35:51,750 --> 00:35:55,350
But the chance of seven without any clue was also one sixth.

771
00:35:55,800 --> 00:35:57,235
The clue changed nothing!

772
00:35:57,235 --> 00:36:00,272
Knowing the orange die doesn't help you guess whether the total

773
00:36:00,272 --> 00:36:01,100
is seven.

774
00:36:02,300 --> 00:36:04,875
Why? Because whatever the orange die shows,

775
00:36:04,875 --> 00:36:08,394
there's always exactly one blue number that makes seven.

776
00:36:08,394 --> 00:36:10,360
So the clue gives no advantage.

777
00:36:10,810 --> 00:36:13,292
And this is the real meaning of independent.

778
00:36:13,292 --> 00:36:16,497
Two events are independent when knowing one doesn't change the

779
00:36:16,497 --> 00:36:17,660
chance of the other.

780
00:36:18,660 --> 00:36:20,196
Let's try a trickier clue.

781
00:36:20,196 --> 00:36:22,830
Your friend says, the total is at least ten.

782
00:36:22,830 --> 00:36:25,902
What's the chance they rolled a double, two dice showing the

783
00:36:25,902 --> 00:36:26,780
same number?

784
00:36:27,230 --> 00:36:28,617
First, shrink the world.

785
00:36:28,617 --> 00:36:32,370
Which squares have a total of ten, eleven or twelve?

786
00:36:32,820 --> 00:36:35,906
Four and six. Five and five.

787
00:36:35,906 --> 00:36:38,991
Six and four. Five and six.

788
00:36:38,991 --> 00:36:42,077
Six and five. Six and six.

789
00:36:42,077 --> 00:36:43,620
Six squares.

790
00:36:44,070 --> 00:36:46,402
Now, which of these are doubles?

791
00:36:46,402 --> 00:36:48,497
Five and five, and six and six.

792
00:36:48,497 --> 00:36:49,190
That's two.

793
00:36:49,640 --> 00:36:51,580
Two out of six. One third.

794
00:36:52,580 --> 00:36:56,363
Without the clue, doubles happen six times out of thirty six,

795
00:36:56,363 --> 00:36:59,740
one sixth. With the clue, it doubles to one third.

796
00:37:00,190 --> 00:37:03,118
Now for the most important warning in this whole chapter.

797
00:37:03,118 --> 00:37:05,660
The order of a given statement matters.

798
00:37:06,110 --> 00:37:10,410
The chance of a total of twelve, given the orange die is six,

799
00:37:10,410 --> 00:37:12,780
is one sixth. We just saw it.

800
00:37:13,230 --> 00:37:17,700
But the chance the orange die is six, given the total is twelve,

801
00:37:17,700 --> 00:37:22,390
is one. It's certain! The only way to make twelve is double six.

802
00:37:22,840 --> 00:37:24,671
Same two things. Swapped around.

803
00:37:24,671 --> 00:37:26,680
Completely different answers.

804
00:37:27,880 --> 00:37:29,668
Here's an everyday version.

805
00:37:29,668 --> 00:37:31,978
Most professional basketball players are tall.

806
00:37:31,978 --> 00:37:34,810
But most tall people are not professional basketball players.

807
00:37:35,260 --> 00:37:37,694
Here's one more everyday example.

808
00:37:37,694 --> 00:37:40,750
In a class, twelve of the thirty students wear glasses,

809
00:37:40,750 --> 00:37:42,580
and eight of those twelve are girls.

810
00:37:43,030 --> 00:37:45,539
Pick a student who wears glasses.

811
00:37:45,539 --> 00:37:47,476
What's the chance it's a girl?

812
00:37:47,476 --> 00:37:51,349
The clue puts you in the glasses world, twelve students.

813
00:37:51,349 --> 00:37:53,286
Eight of them are girls.

814
00:37:53,286 --> 00:37:54,870
Eight twelfths, two thirds.

815
00:37:55,870 --> 00:37:57,475
Cards work the same way.

816
00:37:57,475 --> 00:38:00,743
If you're told the top card is a picture card,

817
00:38:00,743 --> 00:38:05,005
a jack, a queen or a king, what's the chance it's a king?

818
00:38:05,005 --> 00:38:07,442
The world shrinks to twelve picture cards.

819
00:38:07,442 --> 00:38:09,325
Four of them are kings.

820
00:38:09,325 --> 00:38:11,150
Four twelfths, one third.

821
00:38:11,600 --> 00:38:14,840
Always ask, which world did the clue put me in?

822
00:38:14,840 --> 00:38:17,860
That world goes on the bottom of your fraction.

823
00:38:18,860 --> 00:38:20,552
Let's go back to the fair.

824
00:38:20,552 --> 00:38:24,426
A stall has a game. You pay two tokens to roll two dice.

825
00:38:24,426 --> 00:38:27,810
If the total is seven, you win ten tokens.

826
00:38:28,260 --> 00:38:31,922
Should you play? To answer that, we need one more idea.

827
00:38:31,922 --> 00:38:33,000
Expected value.

828
00:38:33,450 --> 00:38:36,089
Expected value is the average result you'd get per game,

829
00:38:36,089 --> 00:38:37,980
if you played many, many times.

830
00:38:38,430 --> 00:38:39,907
Let's start with something simple.

831
00:38:39,907 --> 00:38:42,460
What's the average number you roll on one die?

832
00:38:42,910 --> 00:38:45,709
Each face comes up one sixth of the time.

833
00:38:45,709 --> 00:38:49,608
So add up one, two, three, four, five, and six,

834
00:38:49,608 --> 00:38:51,000
and divide by six.

835
00:38:51,450 --> 00:38:53,549
That's twenty one divided by six.

836
00:38:53,549 --> 00:38:54,520
Three and a half.

837
00:38:55,520 --> 00:38:57,005
Notice something funny.

838
00:38:57,005 --> 00:38:59,394
You can never actually roll three and a half.

839
00:38:59,394 --> 00:39:02,145
The expected value doesn't have to be a possible result.

840
00:39:02,145 --> 00:39:03,630
It's a long run average.

841
00:39:04,080 --> 00:39:07,732
For two dice, the average total is three and a half plus three

842
00:39:07,732 --> 00:39:11,180
and a half. Seven. Right at the peak of our mountain.

843
00:39:12,180 --> 00:39:16,509
Now the fair game. Each game, you win ten tokens one sixth of

844
00:39:16,509 --> 00:39:20,060
the time, because seven has a one sixth chance.

845
00:39:20,510 --> 00:39:23,290
So on average, you win ten times one sixth.

846
00:39:23,290 --> 00:39:25,850
That's about one point six seven tokens per game.

847
00:39:26,300 --> 00:39:28,770
But you pay two tokens every single time.

848
00:39:29,220 --> 00:39:32,960
So on average, you lose about one third of a token per game.

849
00:39:33,960 --> 00:39:37,114
Play once, and you might win ten tokens and feel brilliant.

850
00:39:37,114 --> 00:39:40,882
But play sixty games, and you'd expect to be down about twenty

851
00:39:40,882 --> 00:39:41,520
tokens.

852
00:39:41,970 --> 00:39:45,288
That's the quiet secret of almost every game at a fair.

853
00:39:45,288 --> 00:39:48,400
The expected value is a little bit in the stall's favour.

854
00:39:49,400 --> 00:39:53,909
For the game to be fair, the expected win should equal the cost.

855
00:39:53,909 --> 00:39:56,637
Here, that would mean a price of ten sixths,

856
00:39:56,637 --> 00:39:59,400
about one point six seven tokens.

857
00:39:59,850 --> 00:40:03,755
Let's try one more. Your class sells one hundred raffle tickets,

858
00:40:03,755 --> 00:40:08,110
at one token each. There's one prize, worth fifty tokens.

859
00:40:08,560 --> 00:40:11,496
If you buy one ticket, your chance of winning is one in a

860
00:40:11,496 --> 00:40:14,651
hundred. So your expected win is fifty times one hundredth.

861
00:40:14,651 --> 00:40:15,510
Half a token.

862
00:40:15,960 --> 00:40:19,467
You paid one token, and you expect half a token back.

863
00:40:19,467 --> 00:40:22,770
So on average, you lose half a token per ticket.

864
00:40:23,220 --> 00:40:24,712
But that's fine for a raffle!

865
00:40:24,712 --> 00:40:27,051
The extra money goes to the class trip.

866
00:40:27,051 --> 00:40:29,391
Expected value doesn't tell you what to do.

867
00:40:29,391 --> 00:40:31,950
It tells you what it costs, on average.

868
00:40:32,950 --> 00:40:36,436
Expected value is how you decide, using probability,

869
00:40:36,436 --> 00:40:38,940
whether something is worth it in the long run.

870
00:40:39,390 --> 00:40:41,674
But remember, it's about the long run.

871
00:40:41,674 --> 00:40:43,370
A single game can go either way.

872
00:40:44,370 --> 00:40:46,804
So far, we've always counted outcomes.

873
00:40:46,804 --> 00:40:50,014
But some things can't be counted, because there are too many

874
00:40:50,014 --> 00:40:51,630
possibilities to list.

875
00:40:52,080 --> 00:40:55,375
Imagine a bus that comes exactly every ten minutes.

876
00:40:55,375 --> 00:40:58,328
You walk to the stop at a random moment, without checking the

877
00:40:58,328 --> 00:40:59,060
timetable.

878
00:40:59,510 --> 00:41:01,520
What's the chance you wait less than three minutes?

879
00:41:01,970 --> 00:41:05,267
You could arrive at any moment in those ten minutes.

880
00:41:05,267 --> 00:41:08,169
Not just whole minutes, but any split second.

881
00:41:08,169 --> 00:41:10,440
There are endless possibilities.

882
00:41:10,890 --> 00:41:13,205
So instead of counting, we measure.

883
00:41:13,205 --> 00:41:17,162
The good arrival times are the last three minutes before the bus

884
00:41:17,162 --> 00:41:19,685
comes. That's a stretch three minutes long,

885
00:41:19,685 --> 00:41:20,530
out of ten.

886
00:41:20,980 --> 00:41:24,180
Three out of ten. A probability of three tenths.

887
00:41:25,180 --> 00:41:28,285
It's the same recipe, just with lengths instead of counts.

888
00:41:28,285 --> 00:41:31,390
The length you want, divided by the total length.

889
00:41:31,840 --> 00:41:33,660
And it works with areas too.

890
00:41:33,660 --> 00:41:37,300
Throw a dart at a square board, landing at a random spot.

891
00:41:37,300 --> 00:41:40,720
The board has a circle drawn on it that fills half the area.

892
00:41:41,170 --> 00:41:44,023
Then the chance of landing inside the circle is one half.

893
00:41:44,023 --> 00:41:46,720
Area you want, divided by total area.

894
00:41:47,720 --> 00:41:51,456
Counting, measuring lengths, measuring areas.

895
00:41:51,456 --> 00:41:53,353
It's all the same idea.

896
00:41:53,353 --> 00:41:56,030
The part you want, out of the whole.

897
00:41:56,830 --> 00:42:00,811
Let's go back to our dice mountain, and ask one more question.

898
00:42:00,811 --> 00:42:02,920
What happens if we roll more dice?

899
00:42:03,370 --> 00:42:07,620
With one die, every total from one to six is equally likely.

900
00:42:07,620 --> 00:42:09,330
The chart is flat, like a table.

901
00:42:09,780 --> 00:42:12,314
With two dice, it becomes a triangle.

902
00:42:12,314 --> 00:42:15,080
A pointy mountain, peaking at seven.

903
00:42:15,530 --> 00:42:19,100
With three dice, there are six times six times six,

904
00:42:19,100 --> 00:42:21,487
two hundred and sixteen outcomes.

905
00:42:21,487 --> 00:42:25,078
And the chart starts to look rounder, with the peak at ten and

906
00:42:25,078 --> 00:42:26,020
eleven.

907
00:42:26,470 --> 00:42:29,513
Roll more and more dice, and the shape keeps getting smoother

908
00:42:29,513 --> 00:42:32,774
and rounder. A soft hill with a peak in the middle and gentle

909
00:42:32,774 --> 00:42:34,080
tails at the edges.

910
00:42:34,530 --> 00:42:36,175
That shape has a famous name.

911
00:42:36,175 --> 00:42:39,670
The bell curve. And it appears again and again in nature,

912
00:42:39,670 --> 00:42:42,130
from people's heights to measurement errors.

913
00:42:43,330 --> 00:42:46,630
Why? Because when lots of small random things add together,

914
00:42:46,630 --> 00:42:49,710
extreme results need everything to go the same way,

915
00:42:49,710 --> 00:42:53,010
which is rare. Middle results can happen in many more ways.

916
00:42:53,460 --> 00:42:56,166
It's the exact same reason seven beats twelve.

917
00:42:56,166 --> 00:42:57,290
Just with more dice.

918
00:42:58,090 --> 00:42:59,613
Time for a quick practice round.

919
00:42:59,613 --> 00:43:02,473
I'll ask a question, you pause the video if you like,

920
00:43:02,473 --> 00:43:04,960
and try it yourself. Then we'll check together.

921
00:43:05,410 --> 00:43:09,825
Question one. A jar has four green sweets and six yellow sweets.

922
00:43:09,825 --> 00:43:12,165
You pick one without looking.

923
00:43:12,165 --> 00:43:13,710
What's the chance it's green?

924
00:43:16,710 --> 00:43:18,609
Count what you want, four green.

925
00:43:18,609 --> 00:43:20,751
Count everything, ten sweets.

926
00:43:20,751 --> 00:43:22,650
Four tenths, which is two fifths.

927
00:43:23,650 --> 00:43:27,161
Question two. With two dice, what's the chance of a total of

928
00:43:27,161 --> 00:43:27,600
ten?

929
00:43:30,600 --> 00:43:33,873
Ten can be made by four and six, five and five,

930
00:43:33,873 --> 00:43:36,117
and six and four. Three squares.

931
00:43:36,117 --> 00:43:39,390
Three out of thirty six, which is one twelfth.

932
00:43:40,390 --> 00:43:43,666
Question three. So what's the chance of not getting a total of

933
00:43:43,666 --> 00:43:44,090
ten?

934
00:43:47,090 --> 00:43:49,627
Flip it around. One minus one twelfth.

935
00:43:49,627 --> 00:43:50,610
Eleven twelfths.

936
00:43:51,610 --> 00:43:53,879
Question four. You flip three coins.

937
00:43:53,879 --> 00:43:56,450
What's the chance of at least one head?

938
00:43:59,450 --> 00:44:01,712
At least one means flip it around.

939
00:44:01,712 --> 00:44:05,327
The opposite is no heads at all, which is tails tails tails.

940
00:44:05,327 --> 00:44:09,170
One out of eight. So at least one head is seven eighths.

941
00:44:10,170 --> 00:44:13,203
Question five. With two dice, what's the chance that both dice

942
00:44:13,203 --> 00:44:14,720
show an even number?

943
00:44:17,720 --> 00:44:22,179
Each die is even half the time, and the dice are independent.

944
00:44:22,179 --> 00:44:25,473
One half times one half is one quarter.

945
00:44:25,473 --> 00:44:28,750
On the grid, that's nine squares out of thirty six.

946
00:44:29,750 --> 00:44:33,477
Question six. Your friend says the total of two dice is four.

947
00:44:33,477 --> 00:44:35,340
What's the chance it was a double?

948
00:44:38,340 --> 00:44:42,789
Shrink the world. A total of four comes from one and three,

949
00:44:42,789 --> 00:44:45,368
two and two, and three and one.

950
00:44:45,368 --> 00:44:48,748
Three squares. Only two and two is a double.

951
00:44:48,748 --> 00:44:49,770
One third.

952
00:44:50,770 --> 00:44:55,105
Question seven. A spinner has four equal parts showing one,

953
00:44:55,105 --> 00:44:56,762
two, three and six points.

954
00:44:56,762 --> 00:44:59,660
What's the expected number of points per spin?

955
00:45:02,660 --> 00:45:05,480
Add them up, twelve, and divide by four.

956
00:45:05,480 --> 00:45:07,840
Three points per spin, on average.

957
00:45:08,840 --> 00:45:12,512
Question eight. You draw one card from a well shuffled deck.

958
00:45:12,512 --> 00:45:15,260
What's the chance it's a red card, or a queen?

959
00:45:18,260 --> 00:45:21,712
Red cards are hearts and diamonds, twenty six of them.

960
00:45:21,712 --> 00:45:25,605
Queens, four. But two queens are red, so don't count them twice.

961
00:45:25,605 --> 00:45:28,606
Twenty six plus four minus two is twenty eight.

962
00:45:28,606 --> 00:45:32,290
Twenty eight out of fifty two, which is seven thirteenths.

963
00:45:33,290 --> 00:45:36,711
Question nine. You roll one die three times.

964
00:45:36,711 --> 00:45:39,370
What's the chance of at least one six?

965
00:45:42,370 --> 00:45:45,121
Flip it around. No sixes at all is five sixths,

966
00:45:45,121 --> 00:45:48,615
three times. One hundred and twenty five out of two hundred and

967
00:45:48,615 --> 00:45:52,494
sixteen. So at least one six is ninety one out of two hundred

968
00:45:52,494 --> 00:45:54,860
and sixteen. About forty two percent.

969
00:45:55,860 --> 00:45:59,944
Question ten. A spinner is three quarters red.

970
00:45:59,944 --> 00:46:04,360
You spin it twice. What's the chance both spins land on red?

971
00:46:07,360 --> 00:46:10,254
The spins are independent, so multiply.

972
00:46:10,254 --> 00:46:12,928
Three quarters times three quarters is nine sixteenths.

973
00:46:12,928 --> 00:46:14,170
A little more than half.

974
00:46:15,170 --> 00:46:18,024
How did you do? If you got even half of those ten,

975
00:46:18,024 --> 00:46:21,030
you're already thinking like a probability person.

976
00:46:21,830 --> 00:46:24,583
You now know more probability than most people.

977
00:46:24,583 --> 00:46:27,970
So let's protect you from the traps that catch almost everyone.

978
00:46:28,420 --> 00:46:30,860
Trap number one. I'm due for a win.

979
00:46:31,310 --> 00:46:33,783
A coin lands heads five times in a row.

980
00:46:33,783 --> 00:46:36,250
Surely tails is coming now, right?

981
00:46:36,700 --> 00:46:38,866
No. The coin has no memory.

982
00:46:38,866 --> 00:46:41,890
The chance of tails on the next flip is still one half.

983
00:46:42,890 --> 00:46:44,503
Each flip is independent.

984
00:46:44,503 --> 00:46:47,110
The past doesn't push the future back into balance.

985
00:46:47,560 --> 00:46:51,112
But wait, doesn't the law of large numbers say things even out?

986
00:46:51,112 --> 00:46:53,330
It does, but not by correcting.

987
00:46:53,330 --> 00:46:57,549
Over a huge number of flips, five extra heads just get diluted,

988
00:46:57,549 --> 00:46:59,770
like a drop of ink in an ocean.

989
00:47:00,220 --> 00:47:02,850
In fact, the sequence heads, heads, heads,

990
00:47:02,850 --> 00:47:07,449
heads, heads, tails, is exactly as likely as six heads in a row.

991
00:47:07,449 --> 00:47:09,200
One in sixty four each.

992
00:47:10,200 --> 00:47:11,615
This mistake has a name.

993
00:47:11,615 --> 00:47:13,030
The gambler's fallacy.

994
00:47:13,480 --> 00:47:16,120
And here's a sneaky cousin of this trap.

995
00:47:16,120 --> 00:47:17,660
Judging from too few tries.

996
00:47:17,660 --> 00:47:20,080
A new player scores three shots out of three,

997
00:47:20,080 --> 00:47:22,280
and everyone says they never miss.

998
00:47:22,730 --> 00:47:24,697
But three tries is a tiny sample.

999
00:47:24,697 --> 00:47:27,756
Remember the coin that gave seven heads out of ten.

1000
00:47:27,756 --> 00:47:31,033
Small samples wobble. Wait for many tries before you trust a

1001
00:47:31,033 --> 00:47:31,690
pattern.

1002
00:47:32,690 --> 00:47:36,090
Trap number two. There are two outcomes, so it's fifty fifty.

1003
00:47:36,540 --> 00:47:38,148
Will I win the lottery?

1004
00:47:38,148 --> 00:47:39,976
Either I win, or I don't.

1005
00:47:39,976 --> 00:47:42,200
Two outcomes. So fifty fifty?

1006
00:47:42,650 --> 00:47:46,376
Of course not. Two possible results doesn't mean two equally

1007
00:47:46,376 --> 00:47:49,570
likely results. Remember the spinner.

1008
00:47:50,020 --> 00:47:53,181
Before you count, always check that your outcomes are equally

1009
00:47:53,181 --> 00:47:53,920
likely.

1010
00:47:54,920 --> 00:47:57,440
Trap number three. Adding when you shouldn't.

1011
00:47:57,890 --> 00:48:01,575
We saw it already. Four rolls at one sixth each is not four

1012
00:48:01,575 --> 00:48:03,964
sixths, because the events overlap.

1013
00:48:03,964 --> 00:48:07,220
If they can happen together, adding counts too much.

1014
00:48:07,670 --> 00:48:09,333
A quick sanity check helps here.

1015
00:48:09,333 --> 00:48:12,438
If your answer is more than one, or more than one hundred

1016
00:48:12,438 --> 00:48:14,320
percent, something has gone wrong.

1017
00:48:15,320 --> 00:48:17,740
Trap number four. Flipping the given.

1018
00:48:18,190 --> 00:48:21,489
The chance of A given B is not the same as the chance of B given

1019
00:48:21,489 --> 00:48:24,457
A. Tall given basketball player is very different from

1020
00:48:24,457 --> 00:48:25,960
basketball player given tall.

1021
00:48:26,410 --> 00:48:29,099
Whenever you hear a statement with given,

1022
00:48:29,099 --> 00:48:33,270
if, or among, pause and ask, which group am I really looking at?

1023
00:48:34,270 --> 00:48:38,190
And one bonus trap. Thinking that unlikely means impossible.

1024
00:48:38,640 --> 00:48:41,360
A one in a million event is very rare for you.

1025
00:48:41,360 --> 00:48:43,580
But in a country of tens of millions of people,

1026
00:48:43,580 --> 00:48:45,800
you'd expect it to happen to dozens of them.

1027
00:48:46,250 --> 00:48:49,032
So when you hear about an amazing coincidence,

1028
00:48:49,032 --> 00:48:51,836
remember, with enough people and enough chances,

1029
00:48:51,836 --> 00:48:53,830
surprising things are expected.

1030
00:48:54,830 --> 00:48:56,225
Let's put everything together.

1031
00:48:56,225 --> 00:48:57,750
Here's your probability recipe.

1032
00:48:58,200 --> 00:49:00,864
Step one. List everything that could happen.

1033
00:49:00,864 --> 00:49:04,604
Use a grid or a tree, and make sure every outcome is equally

1034
00:49:04,604 --> 00:49:05,380
likely.

1035
00:49:05,830 --> 00:49:09,320
Step two. Count the outcomes you want, and divide by all the

1036
00:49:09,320 --> 00:49:10,150
outcomes.

1037
00:49:10,600 --> 00:49:13,420
Step three. For not, take it away from one.

1038
00:49:13,870 --> 00:49:18,210
Step four. For or, add, and remove the overlap.

1039
00:49:18,660 --> 00:49:22,119
Step five. For and, multiply along the path,

1040
00:49:22,119 --> 00:49:24,120
using the updated chance.

1041
00:49:24,570 --> 00:49:27,997
Step six. For at least one, flip it around.

1042
00:49:27,997 --> 00:49:30,450
One, minus the chance of none.

1043
00:49:30,900 --> 00:49:35,224
Step seven. For a given, shrink the world first,

1044
00:49:35,224 --> 00:49:36,110
then count.

1045
00:49:36,560 --> 00:49:40,898
And step eight. Remember, probability describes the long run.

1046
00:49:40,898 --> 00:49:42,200
Not the next single try.

1047
00:49:43,200 --> 00:49:47,315
Let's run the recipe one last time, on our game night question.

1048
00:49:47,315 --> 00:49:52,630
Two dice, so six times six, thirty six equally likely outcomes.

1049
00:49:53,080 --> 00:49:55,896
Twelve has one way, seven has six ways.

1050
00:49:55,896 --> 00:50:00,612
Count and divide. One over thirty six, against six over thirty

1051
00:50:00,612 --> 00:50:01,070
six.

1052
00:50:01,520 --> 00:50:04,600
And over many game nights, your friend will win this race about

1053
00:50:04,600 --> 00:50:06,345
six times as often as you.

1054
00:50:06,345 --> 00:50:07,900
Unless you change the rules.

1055
00:50:08,900 --> 00:50:10,406
You can't control the dice.

1056
00:50:10,406 --> 00:50:12,560
But now, you can understand them.

1057
00:50:14,060 --> 00:50:17,811
Look around your world, and notice how often you're quietly

1058
00:50:17,811 --> 00:50:21,466
guessing the odds. The weather, the bus, a quiz,

1059
00:50:21,466 --> 00:50:22,190
a game.

1060
00:50:22,790 --> 00:50:24,810
If this lesson made things clear for you,

1061
00:50:24,810 --> 00:50:27,630
please subscribe to Math Lab TV, so we can keep building

1062
00:50:27,630 --> 00:50:28,450
together.

1063
00:50:29,250 --> 00:50:32,007
For more mathematics, explained simply and visually,

1064
00:50:32,007 --> 00:50:34,300
subscribe, and I will see you in the next video.
